---
/**
 * The homepage.
 *
 * ── 2026-09-06: THE HOME 2.0 SECTIONS REPLACED THE PREVIOUS NINE ───────────
 *
 * Owner instruction, after reading the proposal on /home-2 across several
 * days: "i want home-2 becomes live home page by replacing the current live
 * page". So the nine sections below are the ones that were built and reviewed
 * on that route, and `/home-2` is deleted rather than left as a second copy of
 * the homepage. There is no preview route any more, and that is the point: two
 * routes existed only so nothing shipped before it was approved.
 *
 * THE MESSAGING DID NOT CHANGE, AND THAT WAS THE BRIEF. Owner, 2026-09-05: "I
 * like my current home page ... however we are lacking visual and feel of top
 * 1% ultra premium". Every section below carries the copy the previous
 * homepage carried, in the same order, and the work was entirely presentation:
 * composition, block sizes, hierarchy, motion and the boundaries between
 * sections. The hero is the same hero, with its four-tab rule-engine block
 * redrawn as a workbench.
 *
 * WHAT THE PREVIOUS SECTIONS WERE, so the change is legible in one place:
 *
 *   was                     now              carrying
 *   HeroStack               HeroLive2        the same hero, workbench tabs
 *   ProofGate               Proof2           the same two arguments, unboxed
 *   MarketShape             ProofStrip       the same three statutory figures
 *   HowItRuns               HowItRuns2       the same five stages
 *   WorkstationTour  ┐
 *   ProductScreens   ┘      Story2           the same five screens, on scroll
 *   BothSides               BothSides2       the same three stations
 *   ReasoningTrace          RefusalTrace     the same worked example
 *   Integrations            IntegrationsMarquee  the same connectors and scopes
 *   FinalCta                FinalCta2        the same closing card
 *
 * The components under components/sections/ are NOT deleted. Several are used
 * by other routes, and the ones that are not are a record of what this page
 * was; deleting them in the same pass as a redesign is how a rollback stops
 * being possible.
 *
 * ── SEO WAS CARRIED OVER VERBATIM, AS IT WAS THROUGH EVERY EARLIER REDESIGN ─
 *
 * The title, the description, the canonical and the Organization/WebSite
 * JSON-LD graph are untouched by this change. Changing homepage metadata is a
 * separate, measured decision and must never ride along inside a redesign. The
 * two values below have moved exactly twice, both recorded here:
 *
 *   2026-08-03, OA-33: "for UK suppliers" -> "for suppliers" on the title, and
 *   the description dropped one word. Both scoped the product to the UK, which
 *   contradicts the market-neutral narrative the owner settled on 2026-08-02:
 *   UK public procurement is the PROOF POINT, not the identity.
 *
 *   2026-08-05, A-112: owner instruction, verbatim: "home page must be both as
 *   our bid suite is for both supplier and buyer and also covering public and
 *   private RFP's as well, must cover all". OA-33 had taken a MARKET scope out
 *   of the title and left a SIDE scope standing in its place. The replacement
 *   wording is drawn from the "What is CrowAgent?" answer in data/faq.ts rather
 *   than newly invented, so the homepage and the /faq answer say the same thing
 *   about the same product.
 *
 * OG AND TWITTER FOLLOW WITHOUT A PROP, which is why none is passed below.
 * components/seo/Seo.astro defaults ogTitle to title, ogDescription to
 * description, twitterTitle to ogTitle and twitterDescription to ogDescription,
 * so all six social fields move with the two values below. Passing explicit
 * ogTitle/ogDescription here would create a second copy of the same sentence to
 * keep in step, which is the exact defect A-50 had to fix.
 *
 * TWO COPIES OF THE OLD DESCRIPTION SURVIVE OUTSIDE THIS FILE and this change
 * does not touch them: data/site.ts organizationSchema.description and
 * data/footer.ts brand.tagline. A-50 aligned both to this file's wording on
 * 2026-08-04; that alignment has been broken since the sentence below changed,
 * and it is handed back written down rather than edited quietly.
 *
 * ── THE GROUND LADDER, AND WHY THIS PAGE ASKS FOR THE SOFT ONE ─────────────
 *
 * styles/surfaces.css paints `#main-content > section:nth-of-type(odd)`
 * transparent and the even ones --c-bg, computed by the browser per document,
 * so the alternation is a consequence of the ORDER below and of nothing else.
 * `ladder="soft"` ramps each even rung in from the floor and back out to it:
 * the hard one-pixel step between two near-blacks is what made the sections
 * read as separate slabs, which is the "disconnected" the owner reported.
 *
 * ── THE MOTION IS ONE SYSTEM ───────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * styles/motion.css lands the first block and arrives every one after it, and
 * §THE CASCADE and §THE DRAW in the same file stagger and draw the rows that
 * ask for it. `initMotion()` is the shared `data-lit` switch, `initStory()`
 * writes one attribute for the product story, and `initReel()` writes one
 * number for every block scrubbed by the scroll. Every animation on this page
 * is CSS keyed on one of those, every rest state is the finished drawing, and
 * nothing here moves opacity, so a page with no script at all is a complete
 * page that merely holds still.
 */
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import HeroLive2 from '../components/home2/HeroLive2.astro';
import Proof2 from '../components/home2/Proof2.astro';
import ProofStrip from '../components/home2/ProofStrip.astro';
import HowItRuns2 from '../components/home2/HowItRuns2.astro';
import Story2 from '../components/home2/Story2.astro';
import BothSides2 from '../components/home2/BothSides2.astro';
import RefusalTrace from '../components/home2/RefusalTrace.astro';
import IntegrationsMarquee from '../components/home2/IntegrationsMarquee.astro';
import FinalCta2 from '../components/home2/FinalCta2.astro';
import { SITE } from '../data/site';

/* 58 characters. See the header for why this is a list and not a sentence, and
   for the 60-character ceiling A-89 set. Unchanged by the 2026-09-06 redesign. */
const title = 'CrowAgent | Bid software: supplier, buyer, public, private';

/* 157 characters, cut from 195 under A-89. Unchanged by the redesign. Both
   sides are kept, because that is the point of the sentence and OA-25's market
   neutrality rides on it: public sector and private, supplier and buyer, all
   four still present. */
const description =
  'CrowAgent serves both sides of procurement, public and private. Suppliers answer tenders, RFPs and PQQs from bids already written. Buyers find the evidence.';

/** The WebSite node the legacy page emits alongside Organization. Unchanged. */
const webSite = {
  '@type': 'WebSite',
  '@id': `${SITE.origin}/#website`,
  url: `${SITE.origin}/`,
  name: SITE.name,
  publisher: { '@id': `${SITE.origin}/#organization` },
};

/*
 * WHEN THIS PAGE LAST MATERIALLY CHANGED. Read by Seo.astro into a WebPage
 * node's dateModified, and from there by scripts/build-sitemap.js into
 * <lastmod>. It is a FACT about this page, not a build timestamp: bump it only
 * when the page's content actually changes, never on a redeploy.
 *
 * Moved from 2026-08-05 to 2026-09-06 because every section on the page was
 * rebuilt on that day. This is exactly the case the rule above is for: the
 * presentation changed completely, and a crawler that re-reads the page will
 * find a different document.
 */
const UPDATED = new Date('2026-09-06');
---

<Base
  title={title}
  description={description}
  path="/"
  schema={[webSite]}
  updatedAt={UPDATED}
  ladder="soft"
>
  <HeroLive2 />
  <Proof2 />
  <ProofStrip />
  <HowItRuns2 />
  <Story2 />
  <BothSides2 />
  <RefusalTrace />
  <IntegrationsMarquee />
  <FinalCta2 />
</Base>

<style is:global>
  /*
   * ── THE SMOOTH SCROLL, ON THIS PAGE ──────────────────────────────────────
   *
   * Owner, 2026-09-06: "make sure there must be smooth scroll in the page".
   *
   * It animates every scroll the PAGE performs: an in-page anchor, the skip
   * link, the product story's dots. It does NOT touch the wheel, the trackpad
   * or a finger, which stay native, because re-animating those under our own
   * clock is scroll hijacking and the brief for this page rules it out by name.
   *
   * DECLARED HERE RATHER THAN IN styles/motion.css, AND THAT IS A MEASUREMENT
   * RATHER THAN A PREFERENCE. Sitewide, it broke the site's own certification:
   * scripts/check-sheen.js scrolls a card into view and measures the next
   * frame, and with a smooth scroll the card is still in flight, so the probe
   * reported "no visible box to clip a frame from" on / and /pricing/. Proved
   * by removing this one declaration and re-running the gate: exit 1 with it,
   * exit 0 without. Six browser gates scroll and measure that way, so the
   * choice was to edit six gates or to contain the change to the page that
   * asked for it. Contained. Making it sitewide is a separate decision that
   * has to answer the same measurement.
   *
   * `is:global` because the scrolling element is <html>, which no scoped
   * selector on this page can reach.
   */
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
    html {
      scroll-behavior: smooth;
    }
  }
</style>

<script>
  import { initMotion } from '../scripts/motion';
  import { initStory } from '../scripts/story';
  import { initReel } from '../scripts/reel';

  /*
   * BOOT WHEN THE DOCUMENT EXISTS, NOT WHEN THE MODULE ARRIVES.
   *
   * MEASURED 2026-09-06 in the owner's Chrome, iPhone emulation, on the BUILT
   * artefact: none of the three had any effect, `data-lit`, `data-story-active`
   * and `--reel` were all unset, and the module had demonstrably executed (a
   * second dynamic import was a no-op, so the registry already held it). Each
   * of the three begins by querying for its elements and RETURNS EARLY when it
   * finds none, so a module that runs against a half-parsed document does
   * nothing and never tries again. A `type="module"` script is deferred in the
   * specification, and in Playwright it behaved that way, which is why four
   * emulator passes missed this.
   *
   * The guard costs one readyState test and removes the whole class: whatever
   * the reason a module runs early, it now waits for the document.
   */
  const boot = () => {
    initMotion();
    initStory();
    initReel();
  };

  if (document.readyState === 'loading') {
    document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', boot, { once: true });
  } else {
    boot();
  }
</script>
